---
title: "Project billing"
description: "Review invoices and manage a project's plans and subscriptions."
---

`photon project billing` covers what a project is subscribed to and what it has
been charged. For the card on file, see
[the organization payment method](/cli/organizations/billing).

```text
photon project billing
├── overview
├── invoice list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
├── operation view <operation-id>
├── plan purchase --category <category> --plan-code <code> [--yes]
│                 [--idempotency-key <key>]
└── subscription
    ├── cancel --category <category> [--yes] [--idempotency-key <key>]
    └── resume --category <category> [--idempotency-key <key>]
```

## Review current billing

```sh
photon project billing overview
photon project billing invoice list
```

`overview` shows subscriptions, every entitlement with its version, and
current-period usage. SCHEDULED shows a plan change waiting for the end of the
period, such as the move to the default plan that `subscription cancel`
schedules, as the plan and the time it takes effect. Its JSON is
`{"billing": {...}, "terms": {...}}`: the project's billing overview and its
effective terms, each as Photon sends it, with the scheduled change in
`terms.subscriptions[].scheduledPlanChange`. Invoices are issued to the
project's organization, so `invoice list` shows the organization's invoices,
30 at a time unless `--limit` says otherwise. Its JSON is Photon's page,
`{"invoices": [...], "nextCursor": "..."}`, with the API's `nextCursor`,
`null` on the last page; pass it to `--page-token` for the next page.

<Note>
  The API returns amounts in cents. Human output displays them as decimal
  currency units, so `100` cents appears as `1.00`. JSON keeps the original
  integer `amountCents` and `totalCents` values, so scripts should read those.
</Note>

## Purchase a plan

Categories use the API's lowercase format. Plan codes are trimmed and may
contain any non-control text up to 64 characters.

```sh
photon project billing plan purchase \
  --category messaging \
  --plan-code pro-monthly \
  --yes
```

Purchase polls `PENDING` and `RUNNING` snapshots until Photon returns a
terminal result or you press <kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd>. A terminal failure is reported
on stderr (with `--json`, as the error document on stdout) and exits `1`, and
leaves no recovery-queue entry — fix the cause and purchase again with a
**fresh** idempotency key.

Its JSON is the billing operation it ends on, as Photon sends it: the
purchase's own response when it's already final, or else the operation read
while polling.

Check a long-running purchase by its operation ID:

```sh
photon project billing operation view pho_bop_...
```

`operation view` polls the same way and prints the first operation Photon
returns that isn't pending. A failed operation prints like any other, with its
failure code, detail and whether it can be retried, and exits `0`: to tell it
failed, read `status` (`failed`) in the `--json` output. Billing operation IDs
always start with `pho_bop_`.

## Cancel and resume a subscription

```sh
photon project billing subscription cancel --category messaging --yes
photon project billing subscription resume --category messaging
```

Cancelling moves the category to its default plan at the end of the billing
period, the same plan change the Dashboard makes when you cancel. The project
keeps its current plan until then. Resuming before the period ends keeps the
current plan and drops the scheduled change, as the Dashboard's
**Keep `<plan>`** button does.

Both are plan changes, so they poll and report like `plan purchase`, and take
`--idempotency-key` the same way. Their JSON puts the billing operation, as
`plan purchase` prints it, next to what the change means for the
subscription. For `cancel` that's
`{"operation": {...}, "subscription": {"cancellationScheduled": true, "cancelsAt": "...", "category": "..."}}`,
and for `resume`
`{"operation": {...}, "subscription": {"category": "...", "resumed": true}}`.
A replay of either from the [recovery queue](/cli/operations) puts the billing
operation in the replay's `result`, without `subscription`, as a replayed
`plan purchase` does.

<Warning>
  If the category is already on its default plan, `cancel` reports
  `cancellationScheduled: false` and changes nothing. If no change is
  scheduled for the period end, `resume` reports `resumed: false` and changes
  nothing. A category with no default plan fails with
  `BILLING_DEFAULT_PLAN_NOT_FOUND`.
</Warning>

<Accordion title="API routes used by these commands">
  | Command | Method and route |
  | --- | --- |
  | `billing overview` | `GET /v1/projects/{projectId}/billing` and `GET /v1/organizations/{organizationId}/billing/projects/{projectId}/terms` |
  | `billing invoice list` | `GET /v1/organizations/{organizationId}/billing/invoices` |
  | `billing operation view` | `GET /v1/projects/{projectId}/billing/operations/{operationId}` |
  | `billing plan purchase` | `POST /v1/organizations/{organizationId}/billing/projects/{projectId}/purchase` |
  | `billing subscription cancel` | `GET /v1/projects/{projectId}/billing/plans` and `GET /v1/projects/{projectId}/billing`, then `POST /v1/organizations/{organizationId}/billing/projects/{projectId}/purchase` |
  | `billing subscription resume` | `GET /v1/projects/{projectId}/billing` and `GET /v1/organizations/{organizationId}/billing/projects/{projectId}/terms`, then `POST /v1/organizations/{organizationId}/billing/projects/{projectId}/purchase` |

  The organization routes use the project's own organization, read with
  `GET /v1/projects/{projectId}`, not the selected organization.
</Accordion>
